// Annotated example of a minimal per-project tool-server config.
//
// Key names by client (verify against current docs):
//   Claude Code: .mcp.json, key "mcpServers"
//   Cursor:      .cursor/mcp.json, key "mcpServers"
//   VS Code:     .vscode/mcp.json, key "servers"
//
// This example uses "mcpServers". Strip comments if your client requires
// strict JSON.
//
// The governing rule: every server listed here adds its full tool catalog
// (every tool's name, description, and schema) to every request in every
// session opened in this project, whether or not any tool is called.
// Each server must therefore justify itself against the audit criteria in
// tool-server-audit.md before it earns a line in this file.
{
  "mcpServers": {
    // Example: a project-relevant server this codebase actually uses daily.
    // Recorded in the audit table with a measured overhead figure.
    "github": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github"]
      // Auth note: supply tokens via the client's environment mechanism.
      // Never write a secret into this file; it is committed to the repo.
    }

    // Deliberately absent, with reasons:
    //
    // - The database server: used roughly monthly. Attach it for the session
    //   that needs it, then remove it. A monthly-use server resident all year
    //   is standing overhead in every session for zero benefit 29 days out
    //   of 30.
    //
    // - The browser-automation server: large catalog (measure it: often one
    //   of the biggest single line items in tool-overhead.py output). Only
    //   attached in the frontend repo where it is actually exercised.
    //
    // - The everything-connector: fails the audit's "would you notice if it
    //   were gone" test. Servers nobody misses are pure per-turn cost.
  }
}
